app: RetryingOn was composed on PERMISSION_DENIED, which can never succeed on a retry — a request refused by a business rule is refused identically on every attempt, so this would spend the whole backoff budget and a database transaction per attempt to return the same answer.

  RetryingOn accepts CONTENTION, UNAVAILABLE, INTERNAL — the codes for which the same request may succeed later. The rest are terminal.

  A service that fails to authenticate to something downstream — Postgres, S3, another API — returns UNAVAILABLE, not PERMISSION_DENIED: the two auth codes describe the CALLER's identity, not yours. Return UNAVAILABLE for that failure and app.Retrying already retries it:

      app.Chain(h, app.Retrying(policy), app.Transactional(uow))

  Retrying re-invokes the HANDLER, so your handler must re-read its aggregate on each attempt.